//! # State machines
//!
//! A machine declares a closed set of events, states, and transition rules.
//! `step` mutates a machine value, and `state_name` returns its current state.
//! Every state/event cell must be covered. `default { state }` is the explicit
//! no-op stay rule for all cells not otherwise listed.
//!
//! ```hew
//! machine Counter {
//!     events { Inc; Reset; }
//!     state Zero;
//!     state NonZero { value: i64; }
//!     on Inc: Zero => NonZero { NonZero { value: 1 } }
//!     on Inc: NonZero => NonZero reenter {
//!         NonZero { value: self.value + 1 }
//!     }
//!     on Reset: NonZero => Zero { Zero }
//!     default { state }
//! }
//!
//! fn main() {
//!     var counter = Counter::Zero;
//!     counter.step(CounterEvent::Inc);
//!     counter.step(CounterEvent::Inc);
//!     println(counter.state_name());
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! `reenter` marks a self-state transition that re-enters the state's
//! lifecycle rather than being treated as an ordinary stay. It is useful when
//! a self-transition must rebuild state or trigger state-entry behavior.
//! `self.field` reads the source state and `event.field` reads event payload.
//!
//! Wildcards are asymmetric: `_` is allowed for a state pattern, but events
//! must be named explicitly. For example, `on Kill: _ => Dead { Dead }`
//! covers Kill from every state, but there is no wildcard-event spelling.
//! Explicit cells win over wildcard-state rules. Omitting a cell without a
//! matching default is a compile error, so state and event exhaustiveness is
//! enforced rather than inferred at runtime.
